136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 275 0 R
/ActualText (Part II \(c\) - High dimensionality: Curse or Blessing?)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Most people think that having a lot of data is a bad thing. They say that too much data makes it hard to find patterns and make decisions. This is called the "curse of dimensionality." However, some researchers believe that having a lot of data can actually be a good thing. This is called the blessing of dimensionality.)
/E (Most people think that having a lot of data is a bad thing. They say that too much data makes it hard to find patterns and make decisions. This is called the "curse of dimensionality." However, some researchers believe that having a lot of data can actually be a good thing. This is called the blessing of dimensionality.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (High-dimensional spaces help in several ways. First, it makes it easier to sort different types of data. For example, in medical images, features like shape, color, and texture can help doctors tell apart healthy and sick tissue. High-dimensional data also helps find patterns in data. With more features, hidden relationships become easier to see. For example, in finance, many different numbers about the economy can show how things are related, leading to better predictions. Another advantage is that high dimensions can reduce overfitting which happens when a model becomes too specific to the training data and fails with unseen data. In high dimensions, models must generalize because there are so many data points and features. This makes predictions more accurate and improves performance. Foe example, in natural language processing, large language models trained on massive datasets can generalize better to new text, avoiding overfitting.)
/E (High-dimensional spaces help in several ways. First, it makes it easier to sort different types of data. For example, in medical images, features like shape, color, and texture can help doctors tell apart healthy and sick tissue. High-dimensional data also helps find patterns in data. With more features, hidden relationships become easier to see. For example, in finance, many different numbers about the economy can show how things are related, leading to better predictions. Another advantage is that high dimensions can reduce overfitting which happens when a model becomes too specific to the training data and fails with unseen data. In high dimensions, models must generalize because there are so many data points and features. This makes predictions more accurate and improves performance. Foe example, in natural language processing, large language models trained on massive datasets can generalize better to new text, avoiding overfitting.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
140 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (In real-world examples, high dimensions bring many benefits. Recommendation systems, like Netflix, use many features about users and movies to suggest content. In image recognition, high-dimensional data helps models detect objects or faces in photos. In language processing, high dimensions help computers understand the meanings of words and how they relate to each other.)
/E (In real-world examples, high dimensions bring many benefits. Recommendation systems, like Netflix, use many features about users and movies to suggest content. In image recognition, high-dimensional data helps models detect objects or faces in photos. In language processing, high dimensions help computers understand the meanings of words and how they relate to each other.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Some research supports the blessing of high dimensions. For example, Donoho and Tanner \(2005\) showed that high-dimensional data can still be recovered even with noise. Belkin and Niyogi \(2003\) showed that high-dimensional data can help create accurate classifiers, even for complex data. These studies prove that high dimensions can be very helpful.)
/E (Some research supports the blessing of high dimensions. For example, Donoho and Tanner \(2005\) showed that high-dimensional data can still be recovered even with noise. Belkin and Niyogi \(2003\) showed that high-dimensional data can help create accurate classifiers, even for complex data. These studies prove that high dimensions can be very helpful.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (For further reading, the Wikipedia page on the curse and blessing of dimensionality provides a great introduction to this topic. Another useful resource is the article A Few Useful Things to Know About Machine Learning, which explains many principles related to high-dimensional spaces and their impact on learning algorithms.)
/E (For further reading, the Wikipedia page on the curse and blessing of dimensionality provides a great introduction to this topic. Another useful resource is the article A Few Useful Things to Know About Machine Learning, which explains many principles related to high-dimensional spaces and their impact on learning algorithms.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (In conclusion, high-dimensional data can seem difficult to handle, but it offers many benefits. It helps separate data, find patterns, and improve predictions. While high dimensions have challenges, techniques like dimensionality reduction and feature selection can overcome them. Machine learning systems that use high-dimensional data are often smarter, faster, and more accurate.)
